  'ash-cathedral-chapter-14' => 
  array (
    'status' => 'ready',
    'body' => '[Intro — low bells, ember crackle, distant sub-bass]
Ash on the altar, glass in the pews,
Neon melted into midnight blues.
Footprints burned in a saintless nave,
Silence breathing like an open grave.

[Verse 1 — Kael (low, steady)]
I walk where the choirs were soldered to light,
Where sermons were coded and set alight.
Walls wear scars in a language of heat,
Names flake off and fall at my feet.
Marrow says, “Careful—this place remembers.”
I answer with smoke from the sleeping embers.
My scar answers back with a furnace glow—
A pulse in the ruin that wants me to know.

[Pre-Chorus — ghosted hum, micro-glitch]
Cinders keep secrets when the city forgets,
Oaths don’t die; they turn to debts.

[Chorus — mournful, towering]
ASH CATHEDRAL, BREATHE MY CRIMES,
HOLD THE BONES OF ERASED TIMELINES.
If faith is fire and truth is pain,
Let the soot write what the light can’t name.
ASH CATHEDRAL, OPEN YOUR THROAT,
SING THE BURN MARKS OF MY OATH.
If I was the spark, if I was the flood,
Brand it in glass, brand it in blood.

[Verse 2 — Kael (closer, confessional)]
I find a shard with my handwriting there—
Edges of vowels, a half-spoken prayer.
“Seal the vault—save the few—” then gone,
Heat-warped letters I might’ve drawn.
An iron lectern, warped like a scream,
Holds a ring of keys from a failing dream.
Marrow’s fingers hover, decoding the stain:
“This wasn’t salvation—it was a bargain with pain.”

[Refrain — soft drones, dust hiss]
Even in ashes, circuitry clings;
Even in ruins, the Monument sings.

[Verse 3 — Kael (building)]
A spine of wire through the nave’s black throat,
A relic of broadcasts the Choir once wrote.
I lay my palm where the metal’s split,
Veins catch current, the ember is lit—
Images flare: masks, wings, rain,
The Reset printed in radiant grain.
Not absolution—design and deceit—
A temple to power that prays on the weak.

[Bridge — orchestral low brass + processed choir swells]
(Whispered, layered, not recycled)
Cinders, testify. Glass, recall.
Name the hands that built this wall.
Ink of smoke, page of stone—
Leave no sin unknown.

[Breakdown — heartbeat kick, ash-fall percussion]
Marrow grips my wrist: “Let go of the fuse.”
But the pulse in my scar refuses to lose.
I taste metal; the aisle turns white—
A hush made heavy as pre-quake night.

[Chorus — larger, with countermelody]
ASH CATHEDRAL, BREATHE MY CRIMES,
HOLD THE BONES OF ERASED TIMELINES.
If faith is fire and truth is pain,
Let the soot write what the light can’t name.
ASH CATHEDRAL, MARK MY SKIN,
STAMP THE CODE I HID WITHIN.
If I was the spark, if I was the flood,
Brand it in glass, brand it in blood.

[Verse 4 — quiet fall, candle-level voice]
Behind the altar: a doorway sealed,
Heat-sealed runes that never healed.
Someone etched “Return” with a blade—
The cut still fresh in the charcoal shade.
Marrow’s eyes: storm under skin—
“Open that door and we don’t walk again.”
I answer the ruin instead of my friend:
“Ash keeps the truth the city can’t mend.”

[Coda — single bell, then void]
A breath. A spark. The nave inhales.
What’s left of faith becomes handrails.
I pull, and the ash becomes a tide—
And somewhere beneath it, something replies.

[Outro — long tail reverb, embers ticking]
Footsteps in soot. A key in a throat.
An oath rediscovered, line by line, note by note.',
